8:### 1632 — A cleanup guard that reads the variable the run overwrote can never fire
9-
10-- FINDING: `TASK-260729-1bf72u` revision 1 published a Windows cleanup barrier that computed `$ExpectedTempParent` from `$env:TEMP` — but the native matrix had already executed `$env:TEMP = $RunTmp`. The guard therefore compared `C:\Users\admin\AppData\Local\Temp\TASK-260729-1bf72u-native-windows` against `C:\Users\admin\AppData\Local\Temp` and refused, forever, to remove the exact root it owned. Measured on `ssh win`: `old_guard_passes:false` at process exit 0.
11-- ROOT CAUSE: the guard's reference value and the run's mutable state were the same variable. A safety check is only a check if what it reads cannot be changed by the thing it is guarding against.
12-- FIX: capture `$OriginalTemp`/`$OriginalTempParent` **before** any override, build `$RunTmp` from the immutable parent, and validate against that parent — never a live `$env:TEMP` read. Re-measured `cleanup_guard_passes:true` on the same host.
13-- FINDING: the same block also only ran cleanup on the success path. Everything after `New-Item` is now inside `try`, with `finally` restoring `TEMP`/`TMP` and invoking the guarded cleanup. Proven by throwing inside the `try`: `simulated_failure` non-null with `finally_ran:true` and `cleanup_verified_absent:true`.
14-- DECISION: the guarded cleanup exists exactly once as `Invoke-TaskWindowsCleanup`; the in-run `finally` and the aborted-run recovery path call the same definition. A second pasted copy in the barriers section is how the two drift apart. The harness must persist `$OriginalTempParent`/`$RunTmp` durably so recovery has an immutable parent to validate against.
15-- TRAP: `ssh win "powershell -EncodedCommand <b64>"` dies at exit 1 with a cp866 "command line too long" from remote `cmd.exe` past the ~8191-char limit. An 8,660-char encoding failed; 6,372 worked. Compact the script or transfer it.
16-- EVIDENCE: cleanup-guard probe standalone (not piped) exit 0; remote absence sweep exit 0 with 0 `TASK-260729-1bf72u-*` leftovers; both published PowerShell blocks parse-checked on Windows PowerShell 5.1 at 0 errors with their probe files removed and verified absent. Board outcomes `TASK-260729-1bf72u_runner-readiness.md` revision 2 SHA-256 `d6e1ce11508bd88b8ae356839b071eba75a19e46f434ad53f11b3a47dcc239ad` and `TASK-260729-1bf72u_cleanup-guard-evidence.md`.
